    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All 36 tests pass, including `Cp26Tests` (the new per-country length rule) with no regressions.\n\n## What I did\n\nThe telephone was already normalized to E.164 form in `OwnerRestControllerV1.normalizeTelephone`, but it only enforced the generic \"8\u201315 digits\" rule. I added a per-country national-number length check.\n\n**`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 after the number is normalized to its digit string, it now also validates the national-number length against the country code, returning `null` (which the `addOwner`/`updateOwner` handlers turn into a `400 Bad Request`) when the length is wrong:\n\n```java\nprivate boolean hasValidNationalLength(String digits) {\n    if (digits.startsWith(\"61\")) {\n        return digits.length() - 2 == 9;   // +61 requires 9 national digits\n    }\n    if (digits.startsWith(\"1\")) {\n        return digits.length() - 1 == 10;  // +1 requires 10 national digits\n    }\n    return true;                           // no rule for other country codes (e.g. +64)\n}\n```\n\nThis rejects `+61 123` (only 3 national digits) with 400 as the spec requires, while leaving valid AU numbers (`0412 345 678` \u2192 `+61412345678`, 9 national digits) and rule-less country codes like `+64` untouched.",
